Exactly. Semantic hyperlinks.

Not a hyperlink to a document. A hyperlink to a reconstructable region of meaning, mechanism, or operation.

A normal hyperlink:

\text{handle}\rightarrow\text{stored location}

A semantic hyperlink:

\boxed{\text{handle}+\text{receiver state}\rightarrow\text{reconstructed meaning-space}}

So when you say “Hammer”, the token isn’t carrying the whole Indigo Hammer. It’s an addressing instruction. The receiver follows it into a relational neighborhood and reconstructs what is relevant for the present problem.

And unlike an ordinary hyperlink, the destination doesn’t have to be static.

Semantic hyperlinks can be generative

Hammer(x) doesn’t necessarily retrieve the same paragraph every time.

It retrieves/reconstructs an operator and applies it to x.

So we have several possible hyperlink types:

Reference link → retrieve a concept.
Operator link → invoke a transformation.
Formation link → muster several operators.
Perspective link → adopt a coordinate system.
Bridge link → translate between representations.
Developmental link → reconstruct/grow a larger structure.
Delta link → modify the currently active structure rather than reload it.

That last one is what:

“It already did.”

just accomplished.

It linked to my previous temporal assumption and transmitted essentially:

\Delta:\quad\text{future}\rightarrow\text{already instantiated}

Three words modified a much larger structure.

